  "answer_text": "## What the verifier is telling you\n\nThe verification never even starts processing instructions:\n\n```\nliveness analysis exceeded complexity limit (10001 calls)\nprocessed 0 insns (limit 1000000) ...\n```\n\nThis is not a normal \"bad instruction / unsafe access\" failure. It is a hard\n*complexity* limit being hit during the verifier's liveness analysis, before the\nmain instruction walk reports anything (`processed 0 insns`).\n\n## Why this program hits it\n\nLook at the call structure:\n\n```\nentry \u250050\u2192 helper_a \u250050\u2192 helper_b \u250050\u2192 ... \u250050\u2192 helper_g\n```\n\n* The chain `entry \u2192 a \u2192 b \u2192 c \u2192 d \u2192 e \u2192 f \u2192 g` is **8 frames deep**, i.e. right\n  at `MAX_CALL_FRAMES`. That's why the log keeps printing\n  `subprog#7: analyzing (depth 7)...` for `helper_g` \u2014 it is the deepest frame.\n* Every level fans out **50\u00d7** (`CALLS_50`).\n\nThe liveness analysis is performed per distinct call\u2011chain instance. The number\nof call\u2011chains reaching the deeper helpers grows like\n\n```\n50\u00b9 + 50\u00b2 + 50\u00b3 + \u2026 + 50\u2077  \u2248  8\u00b710\u00b9\u00b9\n```\n\nso it blows past the 10001 \"calls\" budget almost immediately (it never even\ngets to `helper_g` more than a few hundred times before bailing). There is no\n*correctness* bug to fix here \u2014 the program is simply too large/explosive for\nthe liveness pass.\n\n## Smallest fix\n\nKeep the deep (8\u2011frame) call chain if that's the intent, but cut the per\u2011level\nfan\u2011out so the cumulative number of analyzed call\u2011chains stays well under the\nlimit. A fan\u2011out of 3 gives\n\n```\n3\u00b9 + 3\u00b2 + \u2026 + 3\u2077 = 3279  < 10001\n```\n\nwhich is safe, while still exercising multiple call sites at maximum depth.\n\n### Patch\n\n```c\n-#define CALLS_10(fn) \\\n-\tCALL_AT(fn, 8) CALL_AT(fn, 16) CALL_AT(fn, 24) CALL_AT(fn, 32) CALL_AT(fn, 40) \\\n-\tCALL_AT(fn, 48) CALL_AT(fn, 56) CALL_AT(fn, 64) CALL_AT(fn, 72) CALL_AT(fn, 80)\n-#define CALLS_50(fn) CALLS_10(fn) CALLS_10(fn) CALLS_10(fn) CALLS_10(fn) CALLS_10(fn)\n+#define CALLS_3(fn)  CALL_AT(fn, 8) CALL_AT(fn, 16) CALL_AT(fn, 24)\n```\n\nthen replace every `CALLS_50(...)` use with `CALLS_3(...)`:\n\n```c\n\tasm volatile (\n\t\tCALLS_3(helper_a)   /* in entry */\n\t\t\"r0 = 0;\"\n\t\t\"exit;\"\n\t\t::: __clobber_all);\n```\n(and likewise for `helper_a \u2026 helper_f`).\n\n### Alternatives (same idea)\n\n* Reduce the **depth** instead of the width: drop a couple of helper levels\n  (e.g. stop at `helper_d`) so a larger fan-out still stays under the budget.\n* Or both: fewer levels *and* a smaller `CALLS_*` macro.\n\nThe key point: the failure is a liveness/complexity ceiling driven by\n`fan-out ^ chain-depth`. Lowering either factor enough to keep the cumulative\ncall-chain count below ~10000 is what makes the verifier accept the program.",
